WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.184
In this video, we'll mark up
a screenshot of the toolbar,

2
00:00:08.184 --> 00:00:12.367
and ask for one small change,
from the problem, not a solution.

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:15.222
This is a real run, with the waits sped up.

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.335
In the integrated browser,
Add to Chat takes an area screenshot.

5
00:00:20.335 --> 00:00:22.321
Drag a box around the toolbar.

6
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:32.101
The screenshot lands in the chat.
Open it, and choose Mark Up.

7
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.493
Circle what confused people. Testers
thought this lock locked the whole board.

8
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:51.870
Add an arrow, and a short note
with your idea: say what this does.

9
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:01.288
Then crop it down to the
toolbar, and choose Done.

10
00:01:05.973 --> 00:01:08.460
Now the marked-up screenshot
is attached to the prompt.

11
00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:15.272
The prompt points at the brief and the marks.

12
00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:18.796
It asks for the change on a new
branch, and to keep it small.

13
00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:28.592
VS Code asks once whether you trust this
folder. It's your own copy, so trust it.

14
00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.501
The agent reads the brief and the screenshot,

15
00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:40.997
finds the lock in the code,
and changes it on a new branch.

16
00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:47.926
The lock now says Keep tool active.

17
00:01:47.926 --> 00:01:50.771
It's clear that it keeps your tool ready for

18
00:01:50.771 --> 00:01:53.616
the next shape, not that it locks the board.

19
00:01:54.163 --> 00:01:58.321
It checked the build and the type check,
and committed the change on its own branch.

20
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.908
And there it is, in the app.
